Risk / State Management Issue \f1\u8594?\f0  Potential staleness or inaccuracy in grid state if new grid signals are blindly rejected without incorporating updated market information\par
Yes \f2\emdash  your observation is valid and points to a subtle but important design tension in the current grid implementation.\par
The existing behavior (hard reject on new grid signals when a grid is already active) is safe and prevents chaos, but it is also somewhat rigid and can lead to grids becoming increasingly misaligned with current market conditions over time \emdash  especially in longer-running grids during volatile or slowly drifting markets.\par
Current Behavior (Pros & Cons)\par
AspectPro (why it's currently good)Con (where it hurts accuracy)Prevents multiple overlapping gridsAvoids duplicate orders, double-sizing, race conditionsNo way to \ldblquote refresh\rdblquote  grid levels when volatility / mean changes significantlyGrid remains simple state machineEasy to reason about lifecycle (create \f1\u8594?\f0  replenish \f1\u8594?\f0  exit)Grid anchors (center price, spacing) can drift far from current fair valueReplenishment only reacts to fillsKeeps trading reactive and low-frequencyDoes not proactively adapt to new regime signals or stronger mean-reversion levelsRisk/exposure stays predictableRiskManager sees one coherent grid allocationMissed opportunity to tighten / recenter grid when new high-conviction mean levels appear\par
What \ldblquote keeping the new information\rdblquote  could mean\par
There are several graduated ways to let newer grid signals influence an existing grid without throwing safety out the window. Here are the most common (and increasingly safe) patterns used in production grid bots:\par
ApproachDescriptionSafety LevelComplexityRecommended?1. Hard reject (current)New grid signal \f1\u8594?\f0  discardedVery HighLowSafe default2. Replace if betterIf new signal has meaningfully higher confidence / better center price \f1\u8594?\f0  cancel old grid + create new oneHighMediumGood middle ground3. Re-center / resize existing gridKeep existing orders, but shift the entire grid center toward the new VWAP/mean level (partial cancel + replace)Medium-HighMedium-HighAdvanced but powerful4. Adaptive spacing onlyKeep center, but dynamically adjust spacing multiplier based on latest ATR/volatility from new signalHighMediumOften enough for most cases5. Merge signals (weighted)Take weighted average of old center + new proposed center; only apply if difference > thresholdMediumHighComplex, risk of oscillation6. Allow multiple co-existing grids (very rare)Track multiple grid instances per symbol with sub-allocationsVery LowVery HighAlmost never recommended\par
Most Practical & Safe Upgrade Path (Recommended)\par
Option 2 + Option 4 hybrid \f2\emdash  this gives you most of the benefit with controlled risk:\par
